{
  "name": "Gray-Scott Selection: environments",
  "description": "Gray-Scott Selection with each founder's square also painted as its environment, and a weak seed bank (seed_bank 0.0005): a trace of the local painted traits mixed into every new b, which matters most at the thin leading edge of a lineage colonising that ground. Spots keep dying and being reborn; lineages spread across the squares and compete, while the environment keeps feeding local traits back in, so diversity lasts, but unevenly: in the lab 11 of the 16 lineages held above 2% at 150k steps, the low-feed founders and the high-kill corner squeezed out and the middle ones spreading. seed_bank 0.01 lets the environment win (every square keeps its own); 0 lets blending win (two left). Tint = each spot's own feed. Try: repaint a square's F/k (Background target) to change its environment; set seed_bank to 0 to see blending wipe out diversity.",
